From: Daniel Martin <dmanlfc@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dsi: round the byte clock against the DSI PHY PLL

Linux 7.2.6 added a clk_round_rate() on the byte clock to
dsi_calc_clk_rate_6g() and stores the result back into
msm_host->byte_clk_rate.

That helper is reached from msm_dsi_host_get_phy_clk_req(), which
dsi_mgr_phy_enable() calls *before* msm_dsi_phy_enable() and before
msm_dsi_host_power_on() -> dsi_link_clk_set_rate_6g_v2_9() switches
byte_src onto the PHY PLL.  At that point disp_cc_mdss_byte0_clk_src is
still on its boot parent, and clk_byte2_determine_rate() rounds against
whatever that current parent is - it never considers reparenting.  On
SM8750 the boot parent is BI_TCXO, so the round returns 19.2 MHz,
byte_clk_rate collapses from ~72.8 MHz to 19.2 MHz and the PHY is
programmed for a 153.6 Mbps link.  Nothing returns an error: the OPP
lookup still ceils to the 140.63 MHz entry and every call succeeds, but
the panel never lights up.

This is made permanent on this board by
0048-drm-msm-dsi-reparent-byte-pixel-src-to-xo-on-disable.patch, which
deliberately parks byte_src/pixel_src back on XO on every power off.

Round against the DSI PHY PLL byte clock instead - that is the parent the
RCG is switched to a moment later, and its whole chain down to the VCO is
CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, so the rounding is meaningful.  Hosts without a
dsi_pll_byte clock (pre-v2.9) keep the upstream behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Martin <dmanlfc@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -676,6 +676,7 @@
 int dsi_calc_clk_rate_6g(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host, bool is_bonded_dsi)
 {
 	long rounded_byte_clk_rate;
+	struct clk *round_clk;
 
 	if (!msm_host->mode) {
 		pr_err("%s: mode not set\n", __func__);
@@ -684,7 +685,18 @@
 
 	dsi_calc_pclk(msm_host, is_bonded_dsi);
 
-	rounded_byte_clk_rate = clk_round_rate(msm_host->byte_clk,
+	/*
+	 * This runs from msm_dsi_host_get_phy_clk_req(), i.e. before the PHY
+	 * PLL is enabled and before dsi_link_clk_set_rate_6g_v2_9() reparents
+	 * byte_src onto it, so the byte clock is still fed by its boot parent
+	 * (XO).  Rounding through it would return the XO rate, which then gets
+	 * handed to the PHY as the target bit clock - the link comes up at
+	 * ~153 Mbps and the panel stays dark.  Round against the DSI PHY PLL
+	 * byte clock instead, which is the parent the RCG actually ends up on.
+	 */
+	round_clk = msm_host->dsi_pll_byte_clk ? : msm_host->byte_clk;
+
+	rounded_byte_clk_rate = clk_round_rate(round_clk,
 					       msm_host->byte_clk_rate);
 	if (rounded_byte_clk_rate < 0) {
 		pr_err("%s: failed to round byte clock rate, %ld\n",
